# Configuration for the source-built e2e stack (`npm run e2e:dev`) — the same
# deployment shape as the published-image stack in deploy/vps, assembled from
# the worktree instead of from images:
#
#   dovecot         a container, the one deploy/vps/docker-compose.dovecot.yml
#                   defines; there is no reason to run a mail server from source
#   queue-sidecar   packages/queue-sidecar, the same SQS-shaped queue the image
#                   stack runs, over deploy/vps/queues.json
#   migrate         packages/migrate/src/run-migrate.ts, DATA_BACKEND=sqlite
#   backend         packages/backend's dev-server, wrapping the same handler
#   imap-worker     packages/imap-worker's queue drainer
#   smtp-worker     packages/smtp-worker's queue drainer
#   account-worker  packages/account-worker's queue drainer, over the three
#                   account queues — the fan-out one carries the organize
#                   back-apply, so without it those jobs never leave Pending
#   mailpit         a container, alongside dovecot in the same compose file: the
#                   SMTP sink a send lands in, and the HTTP API a spec reads the
#                   accepted bytes back from
#   web             the vite dev server, whose proxy table mirrors the Caddy
#                   routing 1:1 (packages/web-client/vite.config.ts)
#
# What this stack does NOT have is the packaged edge: no Caddy, no APISIX. The
# browser talks to vite, which proxies to the backend directly. That is why the
# gateway spec is image-lane-only — see packages/e2e/src/stack.ts.
#
# Nothing here is secret. Every process binds loopback, the databases are files
# under .remit/e2e-dev, and `npm run e2e:dev:down` destroys all of it.
#
# Ports are deliberately clear of the image lane's (18080/11143/11145/11025/18025) and
# of the local dev stack's (4123/4124/5432/9324), so a source-built run, an image
# run, and a dev session can all be up at once. Seven knobs move them —
# E2E_HTTP_PORT, E2E_IMAP_PORT, E2E_IMAP_NAMED_TRASH_PORT, SERVER_PORT,
# QUEUE_SIDECAR_PORT, E2E_SMTP_PORT,
# E2E_SMTP_HTTP_PORT — and everything addressed by a port is derived from them in
# e2e-dev-up.sh rather than repeated here. `up` refuses to start on a port
# something else already holds.
#
# On a host that runs several stacks at once, set E2E_DEV_SLOT instead of moving
# the six knobs by hand: it names the lane, and the compose project and the port
# block are derived from it so the runs cannot reach each other's containers.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which stack the suite is pointed at. Exported to the suite because it
# matches the E2E_ prefix e2e-test.sh forwards; specs that only apply to the
# packaged deployment read it through packages/e2e/src/stack.ts.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
E2E_STACK=dev

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Edge and origin
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The vite dev server is the origin the browser loads and the suite calls.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN and everything derived from it are appended to the generated
# env by e2e-dev-up.sh, so a port override carries through.
E2E_HTTP_PORT=18090
SERVER_PORT=18091

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dovecot
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app is a host process here, not a container on the compose network, so
# it reaches Dovecot through the same published loopback port the suite uses.
# E2E_IMAP_STACK_PORT is what says so; on the image lane it is absent and the
# suite falls back to 143 on the `dovecot` service name.
E2E_IMAP_HOST=127.0.0.1
E2E_IMAP_PORT=11144
E2E_IMAP_STACK_PORT=11144
E2E_IMAP_PASSWORD=e2e-imap-password

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dovecot — the lane that flags no Trash
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second server, whose folders carry every SPECIAL-USE but \Trash and which
# autocreates `Deleted Messages` instead. Same host-process split as above, and
# the same password and per-username maildirs.
E2E_IMAP_NAMED_TRASH_HOST=127.0.0.1
E2E_IMAP_NAMED_TRASH_PORT=11146
E2E_IMAP_NAMED_TRASH_STACK_PORT=11146

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mailpit — the SMTP sink
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same story as Dovecot: the smtp-worker is a host process here, so it submits
# through the published loopback port rather than the compose network, and
# E2E_SMTP_STACK_PORT says so. E2E_SMTP_HTTP_PORT is where the suite reads the
# accepted bytes back. Both are derived from the slot when one is set.
E2E_SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
E2E_SMTP_PORT=11026
E2E_SMTP_STACK_PORT=11026
E2E_SMTP_HTTP_PORT=18026

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mailpit — the lane that refuses
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second sink, whose recipient allowlist names one address so it answers
# every other RCPT TO with 550. Same host-process split as above, and the same
# derivation from the slot when one is set.
E2E_SMTP_REJECT_HOST=127.0.0.1
E2E_SMTP_REJECT_PORT=11028
E2E_SMTP_REJECT_STACK_PORT=11028
E2E_SMTP_REJECT_HTTP_PORT=18028
E2E_SMTP_REJECT_ALLOWED_RECIPIENTS=^corrected@remit

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth and credential encryption
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=e2e-better-auth-secret-not-a-secret-32chars

# The whole suite is one IP, so better-auth's per-IP buckets measure the suite
# rather than an attacker: ~100 tests each load pages that call the session and
# token endpoints, and every isolated spec signs up its own user (the sign-up
# rule is 5 per minute). A throttled session call is answered by better-auth
# itself, so nothing appears in the backend log — the page simply renders
# signed-out and whichever test was running fails on a missing shell. The
# ceilings are raised here, for the harness only; the product defaults stay in
# `packages/auth-service/src/auth.ts`.
BETTER_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10000
BETTER_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_SIGN_IN_MAX=1000
BETTER_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_SIGN_UP_MAX=1000
BETTER_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_TOKEN_MAX=10000
KMS_KEY_ID=FAKE_KMS_KEY_ID
FAKE_KMS_DATAKEY=8AD6A6C8B5E2488FB01796B662DC01AC8AD6A6C8B5E2488FB01796B662DC01AC

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime — SQLite, the same backend the image e2e stack runs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
DATA_BACKEND=sqlite
NODE_ENV=development
NODE_NO_WARNINGS=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keyword search comes from the FTS5 table and triggers the migrator installs,
# which the app's own writes keep current — no worker in the path. The vector
# index is not built on this lane and no spec asserts a semantic result.
#
# The query-side embedder is the deterministic in-process bag-of-words embedder,
# not the local HuggingFace model. The `local` model is fetched lazily on the
# first semantic query (both /search/semantic and the organize "widen"): a
# ~500 MB download (onnx/model.onnx 470 MB + tokenizers) from HuggingFace at
# test time whose transient failure surfaces the fatal-error overlay and times
# out unrelated specs (issue #219). The deterministic embedder needs no model,
# no network, and no cache; against the (deliberately empty) vector index it
# returns empty semantic results — the same observable outcome the lane already
# relied on — with nothing to download and no revision to pin.
#
# HF_HUB_OFFLINE / TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE are a belt-and-braces guard: should any
# path still construct the local model, it fails fast offline instead of
# reaching the network, and the backend degrades that to empty results rather
# than crashing (see backend semantic-capability.ts).
SEARCH_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=deterministic
SEARCH_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=384
HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1
TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Queues — the queue-sidecar, run from source over deploy/vps/queues.json
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only the port is set here; e2e-dev-up.sh derives every SQS_QUEUE_URL_* from
# it, so an override cannot leave the app addressing a sidecar on the old port.
# E2E_QUEUE_PORT is derived from it too — the suite reaches the sidecar on the
# same port the app does here, and puts an event back on a queue with it.
QUEUE_SIDECAR_PORT=19325
QUEUE_SIDECAR_HOST=127.0.0.1
BODY_SYNC_ENABLED_PARAMETER_NAME=/self-host/Remit/bodySyncEnabled

# The freshness gate that side-effect syncs skip a recently-synced mailbox by
# (production default 60s). The suite drives sync with explicit api.triggerSync
# nudges; at 60s those nudges no-op under CI load and seeded mail misses the
# spec's wait budget. Near-zero here makes every nudge land — production is
# unaffected, this only overrides the e2e stack.
MAILBOX_FRESHNESS_MS=1000

# The send retry budget the smtp-worker settles an unreachable outgoing server
# on (production default 3, matching remit-smtp's maxReceiveCount in
# queues.json). The pollers hold a received record for 300s, so waiting out
# three deliveries takes ten minutes and no spec can do it. At one, the first
# delivery is the last one, which is the branch under test either way — the
# settle, the reason it settles with, and the Retry that follows (#951).
SEND_MESSAGE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Metrics
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The account-worker runs its production poller here rather than an e2e shim,
# and that poller opens the /metrics listener the images carry. In the images it
# binds 9464 on the compose network; here it would default to 0.0.0.0:9464, the
# OTel Prometheus port, which a collector or another stack on a dev box may
# already hold. Nothing scrapes this lane, so it takes loopback and whatever
# port the kernel hands it.
METRICS_HOST=127.0.0.1
METRICS_PORT=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS SDK placeholders — nothing in this stack talks to AWS.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=local
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=local
AWS_REGION=not-a-region
